--- MESSAGE ---
DefinitionPath: the roots are held, not searched

A definition path must mean the same thing projected into Scala, Haskell or
TypeScript. Resolution already did (the previous commit made it unique-or-error rather
than whichever copy the classloader reached first), but the ROOTS were still whatever
the host happened to expose — and a target without a classloader has nothing to
inherit. So the roots become data.

`DefinitionPath` holds `roots: Seq[URI]` and owns resolution: `sources` finds every
root carrying a path, `source` applies the unique-or-error rule. `TypeLoader` is one
line of delegation to `DefinitionPath.default`, so all 117 generated companions are
untouched — the path is a property of the RUNTIME, not a parameter threaded through
every lookup.

The shape is what matters: a path can now be CONSTRUCTED, not only discovered.
`DefinitionPath(someRoots)` needs no classloader. `hostRoots` — reading java.class.path
and lifting each entry to a `file:` or `jar:…!/` root — is merely how the default is
populated, one realization rather than the definition. That is the part a second target
can actually implement.

A SET rather than a sequence, and unique-or-error rather than first-wins, for the same
reason: order is precisely what cannot survive projection. Two roots defining one name
is an ambiguity to report, not something to settle by search order that differs per
host.

A GENERATOR DEFECT CAME OUT WITH IT. The codec gate refused a function-like factory
parameter on the stated grounds that "those have no circe codec" — the right reason
attached to too narrow a rule. What disqualifies a parameter is not being a function,
it is having no instance. circe 0.14.1 carries no java.net.URI codec and does not
mention the type anywhere in the jar, so ANY definition with a URI / URL / File
parameter emitted a codec that could not compile. Nothing in the corpus had one until
DefinitionPath, and DracoGenTest compares text rather than compiling generated output,
so nothing would have caught it there either.

The disqualifying types are now read off `externalTypeImports`, which is already the
generator's list of types it does not own, with `Json` excepted as circe's own.
Blast radius checked before the change: the only other codec-bearing type with such a
parameter is Rule (Consumer[Knowledge], Consumer[RhsContext]), and both were already
excluded as function-like. Exactly one type's emission changes.

Also: the factory default is the canonical `Seq.empty` tree, not the legacy string
"Seq()". The first draft used the legacy spelling and DrakeParseTest's loss report went
[empty-collection spelling: 3 -> 4] — new corpus data quietly adding to #55's pile, and
that report was the only thing that said so.

